This page is not Grothendieck's own manuscript. It is a typewritten page, numbered by hand "OS 5" in the top-right corner, taken from an English expository text on rational homotopy theory. Style and content match Dennis Sullivan's exposition of minimal models (c. 1973–77). Grothendieck evidently filed it among his notes for the 1976 IHÉS talk on the divided-power de Rham complex.

The parenthetical at the top ends a worked example. Take a simply connected space X whose minimal model starts with:
- degree-2 generators x_1..x_n;
- l closed degree-3 generators;
- m degree-3 generators y_k with dy_k = Σ a^k_{ij} x_i x_j.

Dualising the indecomposables of the model then gives:
- π_2 X ⊗ Q has rank n;
- π_3 X ⊗ Q has rank l+m;
- the Hurewicz map π_3 → H_3 has rank l, the image being dual to the closed generators;
- the Whitehead product π_2 ⊗ π_2 → π_3 is given rationally by the symmetric tensor a^k_{ij}, i.e. it is dual to the quadratic part of d.

The main text is the inductive step of the minimal-model construction. Suppose ρ_n : Λ(x; y; z; d) → E is a map of cdgas, where each generator's differential is a polynomial in earlier generators. Suppose ρ_n is an isomorphism on cohomology through degree n and injective in degree n+1.

Adjoin degree-(n+1) generators ω_1..ω_r:
- ω_1..ω_d (here d is a count, not the differential) are cocycles whose images ρω_i span the cokernel of H^{n+1}(ρ_n);
- ω_{d+1}..ω_r have differentials equal to polynomials in the old generators representing generators of ker H^{n+2}(ρ_n);
- ρω_j is chosen in E with differential equal to the image of those same polynomials, which is possible because they are exact in E.

The result is a map that is onto in H^{n+1} and is meant to be injective in H^{n+2}. The purpose is to produce the Sullivan minimal model of a cdga E, such as rational PL forms. Grothendieck's interest is presumably comparison: the surrounding pages compute cosimplicial cochain objects C^n_M ≅ Λ^n Ω_k ⊗ M over a base ring k, an integral, divided-power analogue of Sullivan's polynomial forms on simplices.
